Committed automation runtime authority
======================================

Production event and manual execution now use independent database reads of
committed definitions. The module-global engine and the default service engine
both require this persistent implementation. The standalone AutomationEngine
remains an explicitly constructed evaluator for isolated callers and tests;
there is no production configuration switch to fall back to cached execution.

Events require an explicit tenant, an active non-deleted organization and active
event rules whose current pattern matches. Changes made by another worker are
visible without visiting the rules UI. Uncommitted or rolled-back changes to a
worker's local cache do not authorize execution. Database failures and malformed
stored definitions refuse execution without exposing raw driver errors.

Manual requests resolve their rule and supplied organization against the database
and carry that scope into a separate committed-state lookup. Existing explicit
manual execution of disabled and paused rules remains supported. Event execution
still requires an active rule. An absent organization remains the existing
privileged service scope; the API's authentication and authorization checks are
unchanged.

Each chain retains an isolated definition and revalidates it immediately before
each handler, AFTER any pre-action delay. A changed definition, changed author,
missing rule, inactive/deleted organization or unavailable database stops the
remaining chain even if continue_on_error is true. The chain never mixes newly
edited actions into an old execution. Execution counters and timestamps are not
configuration changes. Concurrent and nested chains have separate authority;
cancellation releases that context. Existing native action author/permission/site
checks remain in effect.

No database transaction is held open across a configured delay or external action.
The action refusal is fixed text. History persistence errors also avoid raw SQL
parameters. Existing history and unrelated application diagnostics are not scrubbed.

Qualification requirements
--------------------------
Regression tests cover production construction, fresh selection, caller scope,
malformed rows, per-action checks, delay-time revocation and cancellation. Private
SQLite ORM checks additionally cover commit/rollback and concurrent/nested chains.
The release must separately bind migrated PostgreSQL and independent worker
processes, the full backend suite, runtime/recovery tests, browser routes, image
scans and package/source checks to the exact immutable candidate. Synthetic effects
must not be represented as live device or provider acceptance.

Cost and limits
---------------
A scoped event reads current active event definitions for its organization, and
an executing chain adds one short scoped read per action. The database is now an
execution dependency. Measure this overhead at the intended fleet/event volume;
a short single-host qualification does not establish production capacity.

Management/listing caches may still show stale data, including after a flush and
rollback. This change removes their authority to select production effects; it
does not provide general cross-worker cache invalidation. The older automation
edit-integrity runbook documents the preceding change and its then-open runtime
authority gap.

A commit can race immediately AFTER the final pre-handler check, and disabling a
rule cannot recall an effect already sent. This is not transactionally fenced
external execution. A rule deleted during a chain can also prevent its history
row being saved because the current history schema references that rule. The
returned refusal remains visible, but durable history retention needs a separate
schema/lifecycle design. Redis claims, cooldowns and rate limits retain their
existing fail-closed boundaries and finite lifetimes. Durable event delivery,
crash recovery, exactly-once effects and multi-host recovery are not claimed.
